1️⃣ What is LinkedHashSet?

--> LinkedHashSet is a special type of Set in Java.
    Unique elements only: like HashSet, it does not allow duplicates.
    Maintains insertion order: unlike HashSet, which is unordered.
    Implementation: uses a Hash table + linked list internally.
    Performance: O(1) for add, remove, contains (slightly slower than HashSet due to linked list).

2️⃣ Key Points
   Feature	             HashSet       	LinkedHashSet
Stores only unique	       ✅	             ✅
Maintains order	           ❌	             ✅
Iteration order          Random	        Insertion order
Allows null	               ✅	             ✅


3️⃣ Example of LinkedHashSet
import java.util.LinkedHashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class LinkedHashSetExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();
        set.add("Apple");
        set.add("Banana");
        set.add("Orange");
        set.add("Banana"); // Duplicate ignored

        System.out.println("LinkedHashSet elements:");
        for (String s : set) {
            System.out.println(s);
        }
    }
}

Output:
Apple
Banana
Orange
✅ Order preserved, duplicates ignored

5️⃣ Why LinkedHashSet is better than HashSet for these problems

If we want first repeating character, insertion order matters
HashSet is unordered → can’t guarantee first repeated
LinkedHashSet → always preserves the order of duplicates